          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8 | Part-II"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.089,
            0.848,
            0.166
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "That is, to combine two different squares, make a right-angled triangle whose perpendicular sides are the sidelengths of the two squares. The square whose sidelength is the hypotenuse of this right-angled triangle has an area that is the sum of the areas of the original two squares."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.752,
            0.17,
            0.873,
            0.186
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Area A + Area B"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.17,
            0.868,
            0.466
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.158,
                0.17,
                0.868,
                0.466
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_9_image_3.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.476,
            0.541,
            0.499
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ Why does Baudhāyana’s method work?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.505,
            0.847,
            0.564
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Can you see why the method works in the case where the two squares are the same size? Does it agree with the method we used earlier to combine two same sized squares into a bigger square?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.566,
            0.847,
            0.607
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Subsequently in his Śulba-Sūtra (Verse 2.1), Baudhāyana provides another verse that helps in explaining why the method works in general:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.62,
            0.833,
            0.697
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "To combine different squares, mark a rectangular portion of the larger square using a side of the smaller square. The diagonal of this rectangle is the side of a square that has area equal to the sum of the areas of the smaller squares."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.717,
            0.538,
            0.738
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us follow Baudhayan᾽s instructions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.742,
            0.413,
            0.763
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "• Join the two squares."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
